      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "You need a wide frying pan or deep sauté pan for the filling and a 1.5-litre pie dish for the bake. A potato masher makes the topping easier than a fork."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Brown the mince"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oil in a wide frying pan over high heat until the pan is very hot. Add the {{beef-mince}} of beef mince in a single layer if the pan is large enough. Leave it untouched for 2 to 3 minutes so a brown crust forms underneath."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Break the mince up and stir. Cook for a further 3 to 4 minutes until well browned all over. Season with salt and pepper. Tip the mince into a bowl and set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Build the filling"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Reduce the heat to medium. Add the {{onion}} finely chopped onions, {{carrot}} diced carrots, and {{celery}} diced celery to the same pan. Cook for 8 minutes, stirring now and then, until softened."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add {{garlic}} of crushed garlic and cook for one more minute. Stir in {{tomato-puree}} of tomato purée and cook for 2 minutes to lose the raw edge."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Return the mince to the pan. Pour in {{stock-beef}} of beef stock and add {{worcestershire-sauce}} of Worcestershire sauce, the thyme leaves, and the bay leaf. Bring to a gentle simmer and cook uncovered for 20 minutes until the gravy thickens and the mince is tender. Discard the bay leaf. Taste and season."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Make the mash topping"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Boil the {{potato}} of potato in salted water for 15 to 18 minutes until tender. Drain and steam-dry in the pan for one minute. Mash with {{unsalted-butter}} of butter and {{whole-milk}} of warm milk until smooth. Season well."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 3
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Assemble and bake"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oven to 190°C. Spoon the filling into the pie dish. Spread the mash over the top with a fork, covering the filling to the edges. Use a fork to rough up the surface so it crisps in the oven."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Scatter the grated cheddar over the top if using. Bake for 25 to 30 minutes until golden on top and bubbling at the edges."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Leave to rest for 5 minutes before serving."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "rows": [
            {
              "symptom": "Filling is too watery under the mash",
              "cause": "Not cooked down for long enough, or the mince released too much fat",
              "fix": "Simmer for longer before assembly. Drain off any excess fat from the mince after browning."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Mash top sinks into the filling during baking",
              "cause": "Filling was too hot or too runny when the mash went on",
              "fix": "Let the filling cool slightly before topping. Make the mash a little stiffer by using less milk."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Top is pale after 30 minutes in the oven",
              "cause": "Oven not hot enough, or too much moisture in the mash",
              "fix": "Crank the oven to 210°C for the last 5 minutes, or finish under a hot grill for 3 minutes."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Mince is grey rather than brown",
              "cause": "Pan not hot enough, or too much mince in the pan at once",
              "fix": "Brown in two batches if the pan is crowded. The mince must sit in a single layer to colour properly."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Cottage pie and its lamb counterpart, shepherd's pie, have been British kitchen regulars since the late eighteenth century. Both began as a way to use leftover roast meat, combined with the potato, which had by then become a staple of British cooking. The modern version uses raw mince and is the more reliable choice. It sits on the school-dinner table alongside stew and spotted dick, and on the Sunday-night supper table in most British households."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}